// LRM 6.6: "Generate regions do not nest, and they may only occur directly
// within a module."  A `generate` inside a `generate` is therefore illegal -- and
// not only by that sentence: `generate_region` is a `module_item` (A.4.2) and is
// absent from `module_or_generate_item`, so Syntax 6-8 gives an inner region no
// production either. The LRM assigns the construct no semantics -- there is no
// value a CHECK could pin, the expected behavior IS the diagnostic.
//
// The inner region holds an if-generate with the NULL block (`generate_block_or_null
// ::= generate_block | ;`) so that the nesting is the only thing wrong with this
// file. It used to hold a bare `I(p) <+ V(p);`, which has no production in
// Syntax 6-8 at all -- a contribution is reachable only through `analog_construct
// ::= analog analog_statement` -- so a conforming compiler would have rejected the
// body and never reported the nesting.
//
// E0228 is that rule. One counter carries it: the parser tracks how many generate
// regions AND generate construct bodies enclose the cursor, so the same test also
// refuses a `generate` inside an if-generate's block, which is the other half of
// "they may only occur directly within a module". The inner region is then parsed
// anyway, transparently — §6.6 gives it no scope, so reading it as if the keywords
// were absent is exact recovery and the file reports this rule alone.
//! lrm 6.6
//! reject E0228
module ch6_generate_nested_region(p);
  inout p; electrical p;
  generate
    generate
      if (1) ;
    endgenerate
  endgenerate
  analog I(p) <+ V(p);
endmodule
